#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171794 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172005 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "747b25be-ff4e-5b1e-abcc-5edd1edd9790" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations That Can Be Modeled Using an Exponential Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "fa51ca96-4ed2-585b-88e6-244b4d8dd66d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16588" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time interval between successive arrivals of customers in a store\n\nII. The number of customers in a store at a particular time\n\nIII. The weight of a child at birth" :question/worked-solution "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Customer arrivals can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next customer enters the store.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of customers in a store is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171795 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb820969-b00e-58fd-9e6d-f8c786f91b33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "4c677102-9f65-58f2-8998-8353f81d84d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-16589" :question/problem "At an electronics store, smartphone purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one sale every $20$ minutes. Rounded to four decimal places, what is the probability that the next smartphone is sold within the next $5$ minutes?" :question/worked-solution "Since the purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next purchase as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/71/714585d89ba3f1d529cd6b64658b778eafa8c346ff9ec592d4997e56803b913e.svg)\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next smartphone is purchased. Since there is a purchase every $20$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda = 1/20$ purchases per minute.\n\nThe time intervals between successive purchases can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda = 1/20,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{20} \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/9f/9f4bcc34982b48061f0fda3c3136e97f37030cb1cf7d526919860ec53274c644.svg)\n\nTherefore, the probability that a customer will purchase a smartphone within the next $5$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{20}{e}^{-x/20}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/20}|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1/4}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-1/4} \\\\  & ≈0.2212\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171796 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "faf45d2f-484a-56ee-af3e-c14a63721c19" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "4baa7bca-acfb-5f9b-a6b4-1fc4b949555d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16590" :question/problem "At a clothing store, customers purchase clothes for newborns continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one sale every $30$ minutes. What is the probability that it takes more than $60$ minutes to sell the next set of newborn clothes?" :question/worked-solution "Since the purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next purchase as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/6a/6a82ddd5e5aa9cc641d3720adb9b998c9bc7e72c4e6edfb929d413a6fc584f87.svg)\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next set of newborn clothes is sold. Since there is a purchase every $30$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/30$ purchases per minute.\n\nThe time intervals between successive purchases can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =1/30,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{30} \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/79/7960b1171c6697fe49628a8f49ac07671d05acf323ebdfb9734175e12a605640.svg)\n\nTherefore, the probability that it takes more than $60$ minutes to sell the next set of newborn clothes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 60) & ={\\int }_{60}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{60}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/30}|}_{60}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-2} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.1353.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171797 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172008 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e40fd627-b52a-5d60-b144-2087979533fe" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities Using the Memoryless Property"}] :question/id #uuid "821b7a41-c7ee-54fc-9b12-b13330b109ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-16591" :question/problem "A malfunctioning internet server approves connection requests continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $2$ requests per minute. If a user has been waiting for $30$ seconds to connect, what is the probability that they will have to wait at least $20$ more seconds?" :question/worked-solution "Since connection request approval occurs continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next request is approved using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the user's waiting time in seconds. Since the server approves $2$ requests per minute on average (or $1$ request every $30$ seconds), the rate is $\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{30}$ requests per second, and we can write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{30} \\right).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s, t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{30},$ $s=30,$ and $t=20.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>30+20\\,|\\,X>30) & =P(X>20) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/30)\\cdot (20)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2/3} \\\\  & ≈0.513.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb820969-b00e-58fd-9e6d-f8c786f91b33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "147b0fac-812b-5ffc-b68e-0bb7f6a18d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2337" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435479} {:answer/id #uuid "2c71bce0-cdf7-58fe-9bdc-53f8437b56f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2378" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435482} {:answer/id #uuid "4786f06d-9649-56ec-9062-82028da85807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2837" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435486} {:answer/id #uuid "5d327df7-7c16-534b-b4bd-6d64d75f1a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2387" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435488} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd4a6c0-5472-5657-8ceb-ca87405f68b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2873" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d327df7-7c16-534b-b4bd-6d64d75f1a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2387" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435488} :answer-field/id #uuid "cac80f7e-d672-5cc4-9408-ca831ac21b23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f0c3a40-75d0-54f9-9bbc-0aacfac8fedd" :question/math-academy-id "q-132551" :question/problem "The lifetime of a certain type of electrical switch varies continuously. Lifetime of different switches of this type are independent, and switches replacements have a constant average rate of one every $2$ years. Determine the probability that a switch fails between the first and the second year. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the lifetime of the switches varies continuously, lifetimes of different switches are independent, and switches replacements have a constant average rate, we can model the lifetime of a switch using the exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the lifetime, in years, of a random electrical switch. The lifetime can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since switches are replaced every $2$ years on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a switch fails between the first and the second year is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X<2) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.2387\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "faf45d2f-484a-56ee-af3e-c14a63721c19" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2601e9a7-969d-56ed-a92d-f155b2b06308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6665" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435481} {:answer/id #uuid "580b5684-e419-5b9e-b864-fa01906c4359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435487} {:answer/id #uuid "89bc60de-fb5a-54b4-85ac-135fedd57202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6605" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435490} {:answer/id #uuid "cf3cc786-c668-51b8-b59b-12ba113e23a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6565" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435491} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd0df45-322b-55b0-9128-385e1d5512b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "580b5684-e419-5b9e-b864-fa01906c4359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435487} :answer-field/id #uuid "24dc1c8a-fb67-5e74-8896-7a038fa53b08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2327e247-df72-5db4-a448-f8b66a015dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-132557" :question/problem "The arrivals of vehicles at a crossing occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $10$ seconds. Determine the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing at least $5$ seconds after the previous vehicle's arrival. Round your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since arrivals of vehicles at the crossing occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next vehicle arrives as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time between the arrival of two successive vehicles at the crossing. Then, since vehicles arrive every $10$ seconds on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ vehicles per second.\n\nThe time between arrivals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing at least $5$ seconds after the previous vehicle's arrival is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & ={\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/10}{|}_{5}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-5/10} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.6065.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172008 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e40fd627-b52a-5d60-b144-2087979533fe" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities Using the Memoryless Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ef2502c-ce4b-503e-bf43-b04378df513f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.393" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ef2502c-ce4b-503e-bf43-b04378df513f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.393" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435480} :answer-field/id #uuid "4df0aa64-eae3-5c33-8bf5-0feea3f9c60f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e0173ce-d070-51b0-81c2-1bf7c8171fcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-254604" :question/problem "Calls to a customer service center occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one call every $10$ minutes. If no calls have been received for $20$ minutes, what is the probability that the next call will come within the next $5$ minutes? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since calls occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next call using the exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the time until the next call in minutes. Since calls occur every $10$ minutes on average, the rate is  $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ calls per minute, and we can write\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. Therefore, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\le s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-P(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-{e}^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ $s=20,$ and $t=5.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20+5\\,|\\,X>20) & =P(X\\le 5) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-(1/10)\\cdot (5)} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.393.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172008 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e40fd627-b52a-5d60-b144-2087979533fe" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities Using the Memoryless Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105e844e-764d-56e7-b3ce-44d005a43128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.982" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "105e844e-764d-56e7-b3ce-44d005a43128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.982" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435478} :answer-field/id #uuid "72ed1eca-9fc4-5b18-a717-801450da7248" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0991a4b-acbc-5194-a13a-e698ad64e9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-254607" :question/problem "Emails arrive in a professor's inbox continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $2$ emails per day. If the professor has not received an email for the past $3$ days, what is the probability that an email will arrive in the next $2$ days? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since emails arrive continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next email using the exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ represent the time, in days, until the next email arrives. Given that emails arrive at an average rate of $2$ per day, the rate parameter is $\\lambda =2$ emails per day, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(2).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. Therefore, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\le s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-P(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-{e}^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2,$ $s=3,$ and $t=2.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3+2\\,|\\,X>3) & =P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-(2)(2)} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-4} \\\\  & ≈0.982.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172005 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "747b25be-ff4e-5b1e-abcc-5edd1edd9790" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations That Can Be Modeled Using an Exponential Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa7c3ede-05a1-5113-90d5-b66b9ef1494b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171328} {:answer/id #uuid "c44f4b18-977f-52a8-81cb-f29961b6e639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171329} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea03852-cd3d-5562-a1cc-69c1267dc51a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171330} {:answer/id #uuid "36f33a8f-5549-5bf9-9ccb-d2fd2c43db51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171331} {:answer/id #uuid "40baea19-fd33-5c85-a140-6d9fd64491af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ea03852-cd3d-5562-a1cc-69c1267dc51a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171330} :answer-field/id #uuid "87ad8b0c-7750-5636-8bb4-d4da84836bbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aedbbdaa-f6b9-52ee-98ba-ab79d3a64164" :question/math-academy-id "q-94945" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The amount of time until the next earthquake in a particular country\n\nII. The number of giraffes in a zoo\n\nIII. The number of defective bottles produced in a factory in a single day" :question/worked-solution "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Earthquakes can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next earthquake occurs.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of continuous time or space.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of defective bottles produced is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "faf45d2f-484a-56ee-af3e-c14a63721c19" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "889010c9-939b-55f9-ad7c-496b3d4079bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0907" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171340} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb5ea4f-37db-54ab-881e-8092d1c3598d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0678" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171341} {:answer/id #uuid "11366ddc-52e8-56d2-a3b8-d9dc670961da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0654" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171342} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca07a65-6584-5597-bc1d-bc0eccef3a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0471" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171343} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8c99df-ca0e-53fa-8fd0-1710af52d767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0845" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "889010c9-939b-55f9-ad7c-496b3d4079bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0907" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171340} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cfdffc2-9fb3-5ca5-a79e-3de9c61edc4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b7dad992-f274-5c2b-bce5-a1d7040d3be3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94948" :question/problem "At a particular underwater location, earthquakes occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of once every $2.5$ months. Determine the probability that the next quake will not happen for at least $6$ months. Round your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the earthquakes occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next earthquake using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next earthquake. Since there is an earthquake every $2.5$ months on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2.5}=2/5$ earthquakes per month.\n\nThe number of earthquakes can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =2/5,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{2}{5}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2x/5}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next earthquake will not occur for at least $6$ months is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2x/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-2x/5}|}_{6}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & ={e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & ≈0.0907.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb820969-b00e-58fd-9e6d-f8c786f91b33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a509034c-c45a-5da3-97a0-63e447121f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3415" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171436} {:answer/id #uuid "b56004d7-7f40-5724-8ddb-6040508abc14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2187" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171437} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8b1bc6-5bf1-5707-a285-a177c1e41795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2652" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171438} {:answer/id #uuid "2499ca89-587f-582f-b791-58c20a72926d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3254" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171439} {:answer/id #uuid "335d2d0e-05de-5522-a50e-86e00f91da1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb8b1bc6-5bf1-5707-a285-a177c1e41795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2652" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171438} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1cb6741-c5bc-5c47-9b89-08e8784f4a69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb32904c-884e-5312-98bc-b88a7bbf47ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-96364" :question/problem "The total time until a computer component registers an error varies continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of once every $30$ days. Determine the probability that a component will register its next error within the next $2$ to $12$ days. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the errors occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next error using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time until the next error. The time until the next error can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since errors occur once every $30$ days on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/30,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{30}).\n$$\n\n In this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that first error will be registered between $2$ and $12$ days is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<12) & ={\\int }_{2}^{12}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{12}\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/30}{|}_{2}^{12} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2/5}+{e}^{-1/15} \\\\  & ≈0.2652\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172005 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "747b25be-ff4e-5b1e-abcc-5edd1edd9790" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations That Can Be Modeled Using an Exponential Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c836109-2926-5149-86de-cfbf47a6956c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435483} {:answer/id #uuid "30f9ac07-0f6b-5c34-a6ab-9f12e00a0258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435484} {:answer/id #uuid "34ee353c-18cb-58d7-b73d-63478f3748f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435485} {:answer/id #uuid "8214dd72-d6d8-590d-81b7-fddc4ba96e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435489} {:answer/id #uuid "fa30ad19-820a-5c33-a201-b90a4102fe1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8214dd72-d6d8-590d-81b7-fddc4ba96e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435489} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f620f94-aa7b-56b8-b107-dbbda4003603" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e53ee3ed-f667-5b74-b94f-f7c404a83247" :question/math-academy-id "q-96677" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The execution time, in seconds, of a computer program\n\nII. The number of students in a class with blood type $A$\n\nIII. The number of books read by a student in a month" :question/worked-solution "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Executions of a computer program can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time it takes to complete a single execution.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of students is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of books is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
